  

<h2 id="because-water-wont-wash-away-pencil-and-top-down-chronological-order-feels-right">Because water won’t wash away pencil, and top down chronological order feels right</h2>
<p><em><a href="https://medium.com/@miscellaneplans/ptpl-229-when-pencil-is-more-permanant-than-ink-and-appending-really-is-the-better-way-for-me-3f6c82ce2155" target="_blank">Read this story on Medium</a></em></p>
<p><img src="https://cdn.blot.im/blog_177bc256f0ee469c998e912e20ece048/_image_cache/1b86f38a-8ef9-4887-a7c7-d7c31552813c/pasted-percent20image-percent2020261019120811.png" title="Black ink sketch of a device next to an open paper notebook, showing squiggly lines on each that represent text or written entries." alt="Black ink sketch of a device next to an open paper notebook, showing squiggly lines on each that represent text or written entries." width="1988" height="1352"></p>
<h3 id="productivity-inspiration">Productivity inspiration</h3>
<p><a href="https://pkm.social/@Ziahassan/116862980175442605" target="_blank">Michael Stevens</a> has stopped writing his analog notes with a fountain pen, in favour of pencil. Because pencil on paper won’t bleed away into oblivion should the pages get soaked, as his did. Even if the pencil gets smudged, you usually have an impression of the letters on the fibres of the paper that can still be read.</p>
<p>Unless you’re using a waterproof ink, you might want to consider Michael’s new approach for the analog notes you’d hate to lose.</p>
<p>I have a couple of completed 5-year diaries written in water-soluble fountain pen ink and am seriously considering writing in pencil for the next one; not least because the Stálogy books bought for the purpose are nowhere near as fountain pen friendly as I’d hoped. If the other inky books are one day destroyed, I wouldn’t be too upset.</p>
<h3 id="adventures-in-plain-text-and-a-little-paper">Adventures in plain text, and a little paper</h3>
<p>After my daily note shakeup in Obsidian two weeks ago, I’ve gone back to appending my <a href="https://ellanew.com/2025/04/14/obtf-start-here" class="wikilink">one note capture file</a>, or 1-N. This file is my scratchpad for the year. I’ve switched from <a href="https://ellanew.com/2025/04/14/ptpl-152-append-obtf-to-craft-a-dashboard" class="wikilink">appending</a> to <a href="https://ellanew.com/2026/02/02/ptpl-193-prepending-daily-notes-finally-makes-sense" class="wikilink">prepending</a> before; both make sense depending on how you look at things.</p>
<p>Now that I carry an <span class="small-caps">A6</span> notebook everywhere and use it as an additional capture point, I want my 1-N to mirror that structure. It’s loosely based on Bullet Journaling in that there is an index, a future log, monthly pages, spreads for collections of information, and daily notes appearing as notes on the next available page. I use letter codes to classify entries rather than bullets, loosely following <a href="https://vladcampos.com/en/blog/2026-07-24-1457-tlsp-how-im-using-tags-plus-a-few-system-updates-and-an-important-correction" target="_blank">Vlad Campos’ approach</a>.</p>
<p>To make the switch from prepending to appending the text file this late in the year, I archived a copy as a backup <em>before</em> asking Claude to re-order each prepended entry in the reverse order.<a href="#footnote-1RG5" class="footnote-ref" id="ref-1RG5" role="doc-noteref"><sup>1</sup></a> As of this moment I’m up to line 7466 in that document, with well over 58 thousand words. The document itself is only 338 <span class="small-caps">KB</span> — super tiny! That kind of efficiency is one of the many reasons I love plain text files.</p>
<p>At the top of my 1-N is a list of the tags I use to classify entries. These are things that have naturally surfaced as repeating themes. For example: people, things, money, blog, travel, church, quotes, spiritual thoughts, French, recipes. Hashtags for things, @ tags for specific people.</p>
<p>The most important feature of both the text file and my notebook is that, other than the initial information at the start, notes are written in chronological order. This lets my mind focus on what I’m thinking about, not on where to put it.</p>
<p>Final word: appending in both digital and analog spaces matches the way my brain thinks about time. There’s a sense of weight in what has gone before sitting<span class="push-double"></span> <span class="pull-double">“</span>on top” of the next entry, and it’s the closest to traditional Bullet Journal-style note making I’m likely to get.</p>
